Short answer: it depends on where the code runs.
UI/browser code (bundled by webpack) — use imports. Webpack builds a dependency graph, will dedupe and guarantee execution order for modules that import the logger. Prefer importing CampaignToolsLogger from the code/common/src module in UI modules and produce a single UI bundle (then remove manual <?!= include(...) ?> includes).

GAS / server-side runtime code (code/gas/..., e.g. PreferenceSvc.ts) — do NOT convert to ES imports. Keep the pattern of exporting into globalThis.CAMPAIGN because GAS final bundle must be non-module code that exposes globals.
Tests (CampaignToolsLoggerEnablementtests.ts) — pick based on test runner:
If these are Jest/Node unit tests: convert them to imports and mock GAS globals as needed (use your test setup to provide globalThis.CAMPAIGN or mock Logger).
If they are GAS integration/smoke tests that run in the Apps Script runtime: keep using globalThis.CAMPAIGN access.

    For jest tests i think i'm ok.

