----
Create a single UI bundle that contains all UI modules and CampaignToolsLogger so webpack emits it once and you only <?!= include(...) ?> the generated bundle. Steps: (1) add a small entry that imports every UI file (and ../../../common/src/CampaignToolsLogger), (2) change webpack entry to that file and set output.library/libraryTarget so exports attach to globalThis, (3) remove the manual <?!= include('CampaignToolsLogger') ?> include from your GAS HTML.
Explanation: bundling everything eliminates duplicate runtime definitions because webpack deduplicates modules. The output.library / libraryTarget: 'this' makes the bundle attach to this (i.e. globalThis in GAS) so GAS code can access globalThis.CAMPAIGN if your modules set it.
Files to add/update:

// javascript
// file: `build/webpack.ui.config.js` (example webpack config snippet)
const path = require('path');

module.exports = {
  mode: 'production',
  entry: {
    ui: path.resolve(__dirname, '../../code/ui/src/logic/uiEntry.ts') // new entry
  },
  output: {
    filename: 'SettingsUI.bundle.js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, '../../dist/ui/gas_safe_staging'),
    // export to the global scope so GAS can consume it
    library: 'CAMPAIGN',
    libraryTarget: 'this'
  },
  resolve: {
    extensions: ['.ts', '.js']
  },
  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\.ts$/,
        use: 'ts-loader',
        exclude: /node_modules/
      }
    ]
  },
  // optional: avoid bundling GAS-provided globals like Logger
  externals: {
    // Treat `Logger` as provided at runtime (GAS). Adjust if you expose it differently.
    Logger: 'Logger'
  }
};



// javascript (TypeScript entry file)
// file: `code/ui/src/logic/uiEntry.ts`
// Import all UI modules and shared common modules so webpack produces a single bundle.
