Agree — dropping the manual include can work, but only if something in your UI bundle sets CAMPAIGN.logger before any code calls CAMPAIGN.logger.log. The simplest, minimal change is to create a single UI entry (or update your existing UI entry) that imports CampaignToolsLogger, ensures the class is loaded once, and attaches an instance to globalThis.CAMPAIGN.logger. That guarantees CAMPAIGN.logger exists for SettingsDialogActionCode and other UI modules.
Explanation: the snippet below imports the guarded logger, initializes/reuses the singleton, and exposes it as CAMPAIGN.logger. Place it in code/ui/src/logic/uiEntry.ts (or update your existing entry). Webpack will execute this entry first and other UI modules will run after, so CAMPAIGN.logger will be defined.
// typescript
// file: `code/ui/src/logic/uiEntry.ts`
import { CampaignToolsLogger } from '../../../common/src/CampaignToolsLogger';
import './SettingsDialogUIFrostingCode';
import './SettingsDialogActionCode';

// Ensure CAMPAIGN namespace exists and attach a single logger instance.
// Use existing instance if already attached (safe for incremental migration).
const G: any = (globalThis as any).CAMPAIGN = (globalThis as any).CAMPAIGN || {};
G.logger = G.logger || CampaignToolsLogger.getInstance();
>>>

// typescript
// file: `code/ui/src/logic/uiEntry.ts`
import { CampaignToolsLogger } from '../../../common/src/CampaignToolsLogger';
import './SettingsDialogUIFrostingCode';
import './SettingsDialogActionCode';

// Ensure CAMPAIGN namespace exists and attach a single logger instance.
// Use existing instance if already attached (safe for incremental migration).
const G: any = (globalThis as any).CAMPAIGN = (globalThis as any).CAMPAIGN || {};
G.logger = G.logger || CampaignToolsLogger.getInstance();



